{"id":4396,"date":"2021-12-06T15:20:57","date_gmt":"2021-12-06T23:20:57","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=4396"},"modified":"2024-10-29T12:40:13","modified_gmt":"2024-10-29T19:40:13","slug":"2-4-lists-cde","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/2-4-lists-cde\/","title":{"rendered":"2.4: Lists"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213ac1141f\" name=\"6a2213ac1141f\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Before You Fly<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-11766 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"106\" height=\"107\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>PRE-FLIGHT WARNING<\/strong><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\">Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong the longevity of your drone, avoid flying into walls or other objects. Prior to takeoff, please review lesson <em>0.2 \u201cBefore You Fly\u201d.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213ac11560\" name=\"6a2213ac11560\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Goals:<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, I will be able to:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Explain the value of lists and how they are used in programs<\/li>\n<li>Define what a data structure is and why it is different from single value variables<\/li>\n<li>Explain how to select items from a list<\/li>\n<li>Describe what an index is and why index values start at 0<\/li>\n<li>Create a program the uses lists to make decisions<\/li>\n<li>Play notes from the CoDrone <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">EDU<\/span>&#8216;s buzzer<\/li>\n<li>Traverse (move through) a list using loops and the list length<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>What are Lists?<\/li>\n<li>Play a Note<\/li>\n<li>Make a List<\/li>\n<li>Get the Length of Your List<\/li>\n<li>Lists and Loops<\/li>\n<li>Additional Lists<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 1: Make a Ringtone<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 2: Light Show<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213ac115eb\" name=\"6a2213ac115eb\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What are Lists?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Making <strong>lists<\/strong> is a way for us to organize information. Some lists can be ordered, such as a list of books you own in alphabetical order. Other lists can be unordered, such as a grocery list or to-do list. Either way, keeping a list of items together makes it easier to access information.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;8140&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In Python, lists store and organize data of the same type without you having to create multiple variables. For example, this list could represent your classes:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">subjects = ['algebra', 'english',\u00a0 'computer science', 'history', 'biology']<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Each item has a position in the list called the <\/span><b>index<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, which is an integer that refers to its position in the list <\/span><i><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">starting from 0<\/span><\/i><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. The first element in the list is at index 0 because it is 0 steps away from the beginning of the list. In any case that involves counting, you will be starting from 0, so don&#8217;t forget!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Having an index is like having the address of where your information lives. A particular value can be accessed by the name of the list and its <\/span>index:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">listName[index]<\/pre>\n<p>Now that we know a little about lists, let&#8217;s take a look at an example of how to use lists in a program:[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213ac1165d\" name=\"6a2213ac1165d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Play a Note<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To explain lists, we\u2019re going to be using the buzzer on your CoDrone EDU\u2019s controller! Since musical notes are part of groups called scales, you will be able to hear your lists in action.<\/span><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;8145&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The first thing you need to do is play one note. The first parameter is the note itself, and the second parameter is how many milliseconds it will play for. 1,000 milliseconds is equivalent to 1 second. We used middle C for our program, but you can use whatever note you like.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try this out and see what the note sounds like!<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\n\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\ndrone.drone_buzzer(Note.C4, 500)\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213ac116e8\" name=\"6a2213ac116e8\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Make a List<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now that you\u2019ve made some music with the controller, let\u2019s get back to lists.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In music, scales are groups of eight notes that increase or decrease in pitch, and they typically start with a C note. What you will be doing in this activity is making a list of notes for a C-major scale, and then using that list to play specific notes.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Create a list called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">notes<\/code>.<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> Next, add in the notes for your scale. Your scale can start with C3, C4, C5, C6, or C7, and then go all the way up or down to the next octave, or the note that\u2019s 8 steps away from the first note.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">notes = [Note.C3, Note.D3, Note.E3, Note.F3, Note.G3, Note.A3, Note.B3, Note.C4]<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Once the list is finished, you can practice playing a specific note from the list. This is what that line of code will look like:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">drone.drone_buzzer(notes[0], 500) # change the index to change notes<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Remember that counting starts at 0, so \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">notes[0]<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">will play the first note, \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">notes[1]<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">will play the second, and so on.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This is what your program should look like:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\nnotes = [Note.C3, Note.D3, Note.E3, Note.F3, Note.G3, Note.A3, Note.B3, Note.C4]\r\ndrone.drone_buzzer(notes[0], 500) # change the index to change notes\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213ac11752\" name=\"6a2213ac11752\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Get the Length of Your List<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You can use a loop to play every note in your list one time for 0.5 seconds. To do this, use your code from the last step, but take out the <strong>print<\/strong> command.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Use a for loop that will access the length of the notes list and then play them for 0.5 seconds each. Since the range of the for loop is the length of the list, each note will play only one time.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This is what your program should look like:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\nnotes = [Note.C3, Note.D3, Note.E3, Note.F3, Note.G3, Note.A3, Note.B3, Note.C4]\r\n\r\nfor i in range(len(notes)):\r\n    drone.drone_buzzer(notes[i], 500)\r\n    time.sleep(0.1)\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213ac117b9\" name=\"6a2213ac117b9\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lists and Loops<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lists can be long, and there may be times when you forget how many items your list has! To help with this, you can use the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">len()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">function. It will print out the number of items in your list.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try it out by using <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">print(len(notes))<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">after your list in your last program. <em>Does your list have the correct amount of items?<\/em><\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your program should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\nnotes = [Note.C3, Note.D3, Note.E3, Note.F3, Note.G3, Note.A3, Note.B3, Note.C4]\r\nprint(len(notes))\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213ac11822\" name=\"6a2213ac11822\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Additional Lists<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Just like variables, you can have more than one list in your program! These are great to have if you have different data types or want to keep your items categorized. For this step, you will be adding another list for note duration.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Use your program from the last step and then create a list called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">duration<\/code>.<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> This list should be the same size as the notes list, so include eight note durations. They can all be different, or some can be the same. Just remember that these durations are in milliseconds.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, add a for loop to play both the notes and their durations, and then run your program to see what it sounds like! You can always add or subtract items from their lists. Just make sure you have the same amount of items in both.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your program should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nnotes = [Note.C3, Note.D3, Note.E3, Note.F3, Note.G3, Note.A3, Note.B3, Note.C4]\r\n\r\nduration = [100, 100, 250, 250, 250, 100, 100, 500]\r\n\r\n\r\nfor i in range(len(notes)):\r\n    drone.drone_buzzer(notes[i], duration[i])\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213ac11883\" name=\"6a2213ac11883\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 1: Make a Ringtone<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now that we can make progressions of notes, try to create your own ringtone! Make sure your program meets the following requirements:<\/span><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ringtone must last at least 10 seconds<\/li>\n<li>Use at least 2 lists<\/li>\n<li>Use at least 5 different notes<\/li>\n<li>Each note must have a different duration<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Advanced:<\/strong> See if you can use a for or while loop outside your for loop where the notes play to make your ringtone repeat the whole sequence of notes multiple times.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213ac118df\" name=\"6a2213ac118df\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 2: Light Show<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]For this challenge, we will use our knowledge of LEDs to make a light show with our drone. To complete this challenge, your drone should do the following:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Have at least 5 different colors<\/li>\n<li>Have at least 2 different patterns<\/li>\n<li>Use at least 3 lists<\/li>\n<li>Have different durations for each step in the show<\/li>\n<li>Run for at least 10 seconds<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><em>Hint: You can create a list for each value (RGB)\u00a0 to pass into the LED functions.<\/em>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213ac11926\" name=\"6a2213ac11926\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 11<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this lesson, you learn about the concept of lists! Lists can be used to store many values of the same data type, and those values can be accessed using index values. You also learned how to use the CoDrone EDU&#8217;s buzzer and program it using lists![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7546&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 12 Before You Fly [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] PRE-FLIGHT WARNING Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/2-4-lists-cde\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4732,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3875,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1737,1782,1848,1793,1812,1826,1575,1438,1252,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2135,4328,14401,2137,2122,2134],"class_list":["post-4396","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-cde","lesson-tag-cdej","lesson-tag-lists","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","module-intermediate","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4396","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":29,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4396\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":13085,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4396\/revisions\/13085"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4732"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=4396"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=4396"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=4396"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}